          <p>The zodiac is the band of 12 constellations lying along the ecliptic — the path the Sun appears to travel over a year. From Aries to Pisces, these are the constellations through which the Sun, Moon, and planets always move. Because of precession, the astrological signs used today no longer align with the constellations of the same name.</p>
        </aside>

        <section id="intro" class="content-section">
          <h2>Introduction to the Zodiac</h2>
          <p>The word "zodiac" comes from the Greek <em>zodiakos kyklos</em>, meaning "circle of little animals" — a reference to the mostly living creatures that populate this band of sky. For at least three thousand years, the 12 zodiac constellations have held a special place in human culture, because they mark the highway travelled by the Sun, Moon, and planets across the celestial sphere.</p>
          <p>Unlike the arbitrary groupings elsewhere in the sky, the zodiac constellations share a defining geometric property: they all straddle the ecliptic, the great circle that represents the plane of Earth's orbit projected onto the stars. This is why the zodiac mattered so much to ancient sky-watchers. If you wanted to predict where the wandering planets would appear, you only had to watch these twelve patterns.</p>
          <p>Today the zodiac occupies a curious dual identity. To astronomers it is simply the set of constellations along the ecliptic — a useful reference band for tracking the Solar System's objects. To the older tradition of astrology it is a system of 12 equal "signs" that, due to the slow drift of precession, have gradually parted company with the actual stars they were named after.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="ecliptic" class="content-section">
          <h2>The Ecliptic: The Sun's Road</h2>
          <p>The ecliptic is the apparent yearly path of the Sun against the background stars. As Earth orbits the Sun, the Sun appears to move eastward through the constellations, completing one full circuit in 365.25 days and advancing by roughly one degree per day. Because Earth's axis is tilted 23.4 degrees relative to its orbit, the ecliptic is inclined by that same angle to the celestial equator.</p>
          <p>The Moon and the eight planets all orbit the Sun in nearly the same plane, so from Earth they too are always found within a few degrees of the ecliptic — a strip of sky about 16 degrees wide known as the zodiac belt. This is why you will never see Jupiter or Saturn near the celestial pole; the planets are permanently confined to the zodiac.</p>
          <div class="info-box glass">
            <h3>Key Points on the Ecliptic</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Vernal (spring) equinox:</strong> The Sun crosses the equator heading north, currently located in Pisces</li>
              <li><strong>Summer solstice:</strong> The Sun reaches its northernmost point, in Taurus/Gemini</li>
              <li><strong>Autumnal equinox:</strong> The Sun crosses the equator heading south, in Virgo</li>
              <li><strong>Winter solstice:</strong> The Sun reaches its southernmost point, in Sagittarius</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>Eclipses take their name from the ecliptic because they can only occur when the new or full Moon lies exactly on this line, allowing the Sun, Earth, and Moon to align. The Moon's own orbit is tilted about 5 degrees, so most months it passes above or below the Sun rather than directly across it.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="twelve" class="content-section">
          <h2>The Twelve Zodiac Constellations</h2>
          <p>Below are the 12 traditional zodiac constellations in the order the Sun passes through them, along with their brightest stars and the season when they are best seen in the evening sky.</p>
          <div class="info-box glass">
            <h3>The Zodiac in Order</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Aries (The Ram):</strong> Small and faint; brightest star Hamal. Best seen on autumn evenings.</li>
              <li><strong>Taurus (The Bull):</strong> Marked by orange Aldebaran and the Pleiades and Hyades clusters. Winter.</li>
              <li><strong>Gemini (The Twins):</strong> Headed by the bright pair Castor and Pollux. Winter.</li>
              <li><strong>Cancer (The Crab):</strong> The faintest zodiac constellation; hosts the Beehive Cluster (M44). Spring.</li>
              <li><strong>Leo (The Lion):</strong> A true lion shape anchored by blue-white Regulus. Spring.</li>
              <li><strong>Virgo (The Maiden):</strong> The largest zodiac constellation; bright star Spica. Spring.</li>
              <li><strong>Libra (The Scales):</strong> The only inanimate zodiac sign; once the claws of Scorpius. Summer.</li>
              <li><strong>Scorpius (The Scorpion):</strong> A vivid J-shaped scorpion led by red Antares. Summer.</li>
              <li><strong>Sagittarius (The Archer):</strong> The "teapot" pointing to the galactic center. Summer.</li>
              <li><strong>Capricornus (The Sea-Goat):</strong> A faint triangular pattern; ancient origins. Autumn.</li>
              <li><strong>Aquarius (The Water Bearer):</strong> Large but dim; contains the Helix Nebula. Autumn.</li>
              <li><strong>Pisces (The Fishes):</strong> A faint V of two fish; holds the current vernal equinox. Autumn.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <h3>Bright and Faint Members</h3>
          <p>The zodiac is a study in contrasts. Some of its members — Leo, Taurus, Scorpius, and Gemini — are among the most striking and easily recognized constellations in the whole sky, packed with first-magnitude stars. Others — Cancer, Libra, Capricornus, and Pisces — are so faint that they can be difficult to make out from a light-polluted location, and beginners often struggle to trace their shapes without a star chart.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="ophiuchus" class="content-section">
          <h2>Ophiuchus: The Forgotten 13th</h2>
          <p>Although tradition fixes the zodiac at 12 constellations, the Sun actually spends about 18 days each year — from roughly November 30 to December 17 — within the boundaries of Ophiuchus, the Serpent Bearer. This large constellation dips its foot across the ecliptic between Scorpius and Sagittarius.</p>
          <p>Ophiuchus was excluded from the zodiac not by accident but by design. When Babylonian astronomers laid out the zodiac, they wanted a tidy system of 12 equal segments of 30 degrees each, matching the 12 months of the lunar calendar. The constellations were of unequal size, so the neat 30-degree "signs" of astrology never corresponded exactly to the messy real constellations even at the outset.</p>
          <p>Periodically the media rediscovers Ophiuchus and announces that the zodiac has "changed" to include a 13th sign. In reality nothing has changed — Ophiuchus has always straddled the ecliptic, and astrology has always chosen to ignore it. The story simply highlights the gap between the constellations as astronomers define them and the idealized 12-sign scheme of astrology.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="precession" class="content-section">
          <h2>Precession and the Astrology Disconnect</h2>
          <p>Earth's rotational axis does not point in a fixed direction. Like a spinning top slowing down, it traces a slow circle in the sky over a period of about 25,772 years — a motion called axial precession. As the axis wanders, the position of the equinoxes slides westward along the ecliptic at a rate of about one degree every 72 years.</p>
          <p>When Greek and Babylonian astronomers codified the zodiac around 2,000 years ago, the vernal equinox sat at the border of Aries, and the astrological signs were defined to begin there. Since then, precession has carried the equinox backward all the way through Pisces. As a result, the astrological sign and the actual constellation are now offset by nearly a full sign.</p>
          <div class="info-box glass">
            <h3>Sign vs. Constellation Today</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Someone told they are "a Leo" (Sun-sign) actually had the Sun in front of Cancer at birth</li>
              <li>The astrological signs track the <strong>seasons</strong> (tropical zodiac), fixed to the equinoxes</li>
              <li>The constellations track the <strong>stars</strong> (sidereal zodiac), which precession moves</li>
              <li>In roughly 12,000 years, Vega will replace Polaris as the northern pole star — the same precession at work</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>It is important to stress that this is not a criticism of anyone's beliefs but simply a description of the astronomy: the tropical zodiac used in Western astrology was deliberately anchored to the seasons rather than the stars, so the two systems were always destined to drift apart.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="observation" class="content-section">
          <h2>Observing the Zodiac</h2>
          <p>Because the zodiac forms a continuous band circling the entire sky, some portion of it is always visible on any clear night. Tracing the ecliptic by eye is one of the most rewarding skills a beginner can learn, because it reveals the hidden order behind the wandering planets.</p>
          <h3>How to Follow the Ecliptic</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Find a bright planet:</strong> Venus, Jupiter, Mars, and Saturn all lie on the ecliptic. Two or more planets in the sky instantly sketch out the line for you.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch the Moon:</strong> Over a week, note how the Moon moves eastward through successive zodiac constellations — it advances about 13 degrees per night.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the seasons:</strong> Learn the two or three zodiac constellations best placed each season and build outward from there.</li>
            <li><strong>Start with the showpieces:</strong> Taurus, Gemini, Leo, and Scorpius are easy anchors; use them to locate their fainter neighbors.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Deep-Sky Treasures Along the Zodiac</h3>
          <p>The zodiac is rich in celestial objects for binoculars and telescopes. Taurus holds the Pleiades (M45) and the Crab Nebula (M1). Cancer contains the Beehive Cluster (M44). Sagittarius, gazing toward the heart of the Milky Way, is arguably the single richest constellation in the sky, packed with the Lagoon, Trifid, and Omega nebulae and dozens of star clusters. Virgo lies toward a great cluster of galaxies visible in modest telescopes.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="facts" class="content-section">
          <h2>Interesting Facts About the Zodiac</h2>
          <ul class="facts-list">
            <li><strong>Libra was once part of Scorpius:</strong> The Romans split off the scorpion's claws to create the Scales. The old link survives in the star names Zubenelgenubi and Zubeneschamali — Arabic for the "southern claw" and "northern claw."</li>
            <li><strong>The only non-living sign:</strong> Libra is the sole zodiac constellation representing an inanimate object rather than an animal or person, making the Greek name "circle of animals" slightly inaccurate.</li>
            <li><strong>Sagittarius points to the galactic center:</strong> The supermassive black hole at the heart of the Milky Way, Sagittarius A*, lies in the direction of this constellation, roughly 26,000 light-years away.</li>
            <li><strong>Aquarius hosts a dying Sun's future:</strong> The Helix Nebula in Aquarius is one of the closest planetary nebulae — a preview of what our own Sun will become in about five billion years.</li>
            <li><strong>The equinox is leaving Pisces:</strong> The vernal equinox point is slowly moving from Pisces toward Aquarius, which is the astronomical basis for the poetic phrase "the dawning of the Age of Aquarius."</li>
            <li><strong>Zodiacal light:</strong> On very dark spring or autumn evenings, a faint pyramid of light called the zodiacal light can be seen along the ecliptic — sunlight scattered by dust grains lying in the plane of the Solar System.</li>
            <li><strong>Ancient consistency:</strong> The zodiac is one of the oldest surviving scientific constructs; the Babylonian star catalog MUL.APIN from around 1000 BCE already lists constellations recognizably ancestral to today's zodiac.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="resources" class="content-section">
          <h2>External Resources</h2>
          <ul class="resource-list">
            <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Solar System</a> — How the planets move along the ecliptic through the zodiac</li>
